From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifimngr: event rearm pace
Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

Pace the ENETDOWN event-fd re-arm to stop a boot-time fd-exhaustion spin.

Root cause (HW-confirmed 2026-07-15): wifimngr_event_uloop_cb() re-armed the
uloop fd synchronously (uloop_fd_delete + uloop_fd_add ULOOP_EDGE_TRIGGER) from
inside the read callback whenever wifi_recv_event() returned -ENETDOWN. On a
down/flapping event socket the fd is instantly "readable" (EOF/error), so the
edge re-fires as fast as uloop can cycle, and every firing re-runs the libwifi
event-source reopen (parent socket + assoclist/status seed opens added by the
apconn/staconn event caches). During the boot BSS bring-up storm this bursts the
system-wide file table to ENFILE ("Too many open files in system"), which makes
hostapd add_iface fail for the 6 GHz radio and prevents the EasyMesh backhaul
from forming. The per-source WPACTRL_MAX_FAILS/dead bound does not help because
the tight re-arm is above it and a flapping socket never accumulates 5
consecutive failures.

Fix: re-arm on a short uloop_timeout (500 ms) instead of synchronously, bounding
the whole-process reopen rate regardless of how many sources flap. Source-
agnostic: every event source (netlink and the wpactrl/hapdctrl caches) routes
through this callback. The pending timer is cancelled in wifimngr_event_exit()
so a reload cannot fire it on freed memory.

--- a/wifimngr_event.c
+++ b/wifimngr_event.c
@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@
 
 struct wifimngr_event {
 	struct uloop_fd uloop_fd;
+	struct uloop_timeout rearm;	/* 910: paced ENETDOWN re-arm (anti fd-exhaustion) */
 	struct list_head list;
 	struct event_struct event;
 	void *handle;
@@ -136,6 +137,25 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* 910 fix: pace the ENETDOWN re-arm so a down/flapping event socket cannot
+ * spin the reopen path into fd exhaustion. The old code re-armed the uloop fd
+ * synchronously (delete + add EDGE_TRIGGER) from inside the read callback; on a
+ * socket that is down the fd is instantly "readable" (EOF/error), so the edge
+ * re-fires as fast as uloop can cycle and each firing re-runs the libwifi
+ * event-source reopen (parent + seed opens). During the boot BSS bring-up storm
+ * that bursts the system file table to ENFILE ("Too many open files"), which
+ * kills 6 GHz iface creation and stops the mesh backhaul from forming. Re-arming
+ * on a short timer bounds the whole-process reopen rate regardless of how many
+ * sources flap. */
+#define WIFIMNGR_EVENT_REARM_MS		500
+
+static void wifimngr_event_rearm_cb(struct uloop_timeout *t)
+{
+	struct wifimngr_event *ev = container_of(t, struct wifimngr_event, rearm);
+
+	uloop_fd_add(&ev->uloop_fd, ULOOP_READ | ULOOP_EDGE_TRIGGER);
+}
+
 static void wifimngr_event_uloop_cb(struct uloop_fd *fd, unsigned int events)
 {
 	struct wifimngr_event *ev = (void *) fd;
@@ -148,9 +168,10 @@
 	}
 
 	if (rv == -ENETDOWN) {
-		wifimngr_dbg("%s read error ENETDOWN - rearm uloop fd monitor\n", ev->event.ifname);
+		wifimngr_dbg("%s read error ENETDOWN - rearm uloop fd monitor (paced)\n", ev->event.ifname);
 		uloop_fd_delete(&ev->uloop_fd);
-		uloop_fd_add(&ev->uloop_fd, ULOOP_READ | ULOOP_EDGE_TRIGGER);
+		ev->rearm.cb = wifimngr_event_rearm_cb;
+		uloop_timeout_set(&ev->rearm, WIFIMNGR_EVENT_REARM_MS);
 	}
 }
 
@@ -161,6 +182,7 @@
 	list_for_each_entry_safe(ev, tmp, &w->event_list, list) {
 		wifimngr_dbg("remove %s %s %s\n", ev->event.ifname, ev->event.family, ev->event.group);
 		uloop_fd_delete(&ev->uloop_fd);
+		uloop_timeout_cancel(&ev->rearm);
 		wifi_unregister_event(ev->event.ifname, ev->handle);
 		list_del(&ev->list);
 		free(ev->event.resp.data);
